===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|chery}}, {{m|enm|cherie}}, {{m|enm|chirie}}, from {{der|en|xno|cherise}} (mistaken as a plural) and {{inh|en|ang|ċiris}}, {{m|ang|ċirse|t=cherry}}, both ultimately from {{der|en|VL.|ceresia}}, derived from {{der|en|LL.|ceresium}}, {{m|la|cerasium}}, from {{der|en|grc|κεράσιον|t=cherry fruit}}, from {{m|grc|κερασός||[[bird cherry]]}}, and ultimately possibly of {{der|en|ine-ana|-}} origin (the intervocalic {{lang|grc|σ}} suggests a pre-Greek origin for the word).<ref name="Beekes2010">{{cite-book|author=Robert Stephen Paul Beekes|title=Etymological Dictionary of Greek|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=CltlewAACAAJ| year=2010| publisher=Brill| entry=κέρασος|isbn=978-90-04-17418-4}}</ref> {{doublet|en|cerise|kirsch}}.

